luding six key IEA recommendations for a new, comprehensive approach to mineral security.--------------------------------------------------------------------------------\nThe Role of Critical Minerals in Clean Energy Transitions – Analysis - IEA (https://www.iea.org/articles/the-role-of-critical-minerals-in-clean-energy-transitions)\nciteturn123search2 [wordlim: 200] Published: 5.5 years ago; Crawled: today; \n\n# The Role of Critical Minerals in Clean Energy Transitions\n\nThis report is part of World Energy Outlook\n\nPublished 05 May 2021\n\nLicence CC BY 4.0--------------------------------------------------------------------------------\nThe Role of Critical Minerals in Clean Energy Transitions – Analysis - IEA (https://www.iea.org/commentaries/the-role-of-critical-minerals-in-clean-energy-transitions)\nciteturn123search3 [wordlim: 200] Published: 5.5 years ago; Crawled: 3 weeks ago; \n\n# The Role of Critical Minerals in Clean Energy Transitions\n\nReport — 05 May 2021\n\nCite Share\n\n#### Cite commentary\n\nIEA (2021), The Role of Critical Minerals in Clean Energy Transitions, IEA, Paris https://www.iea.org/commentaries/the-role-of-critical-minerals-in-clean-energy-transitions, Licence: CC BY 4.0--------------------------------------------------------------------------------\nSummary and critical review of the International Energy Agency’s special report: The role of critical minerals in clean energy transitions (https://revistademetalurgia.revistas.csic.es/index.php/revistademetalurgia/article/download/1514/1931)\nciteturn123search4 [wordlim: 200] Published: 5.3 years ago; Crawled: today; \nRevista de Metalurgia 57 (2)\n\nApril-June 2021, e197\n\nISSN-L: 0034-8570, eISSN: 1988-4222\n\nhttps://doi.org/10.3989/revmetalm.197\n\n# Summary and critical review of the International Energy Agency’s special report: The role of critical minerals in clean energy transitions\n\n## Resumen y análisis crítico del informe especial de la Agencia Internacional de la Energía: El Rol de los minerales críticos en la transición hacia energías limpias\n\nAlicia Valero\n\nInstituto CIRCE- Universidad de Zaragoza. Mariano Esquillor Gómez, 15. 50.011 Zaragoza, Spain\n\nhttps://orcid.org/0000-0003-3330-1793\n\naliciavd@unizar.es\n\nAntonio Valero\n\nInstituto CIRCE- Universidad de Zaragoza. Mariano Esquillor Gómez, 15. 50.011 Zaragoza, Spain\n\nhttps://orcid.org/0000-0003-0702-733X\n\nGuiomar Calvo\n\nInstituto CIRCE- Universidad de Zaragoza. Mariano Esquillor Gómez, 15. 50.011 Zaragoza, Spain\n\nhttps://orcid.org/0000-0001-9263-7321\n\nABSTRACT\n\nSource: IEA (2021a)IEA (2021a). The Role of Critical Minerals in Clean Energy Transitions. World Energy Outlook Special Report. International Energy Agency.https://iea.blob.core.windows.net/assets/24d5dfbb-a77a-4647-abcc-667867207f74/TheRoleofCriticalMineralsinCleanEnergyTransitions.pdf.. The Role of Critical Minerals in the Energy Transition. All rights reserved.\n\nThat said, the IEA also recognises that energy transition minerals involve higher GHG emission intensities. For example, producing neodymium oxide implies near 80 t CO_{2}-eq/ton, for cobalt sulphate over 15, whereas for iron and steel about 2 t CO_{2}-eq/ton (see Fig. 4). Moreover, as demand for energy transition minerals spirals up, new resources and processing routes with higher GHG emissions intensities will increase their share. For example, more and more nickel will need to be produced from laterites instead of from sulphide ores. Depending on the processing route (matte via nickel pig iron or high-pressure acid leaching), emissions increase between 1.5 and 5 times respectively, when compared to nickel production from sulphides. --------------------------------------------------------------------------------\nExecutive summary - The Role of Critical Minerals in Clean Energy Transitions - IEA (https://www.iea.org/reports/the-role-of-critical-minerals-in-clean-energy-transitions/executive-summary)\nciteturn123search5 [wordlim: 200] Published: 5.5 years ago; Crawled: today; \n\n### IEA’s six key recommendations for a new, comprehensive approach to mineral security\n\n1. Ensure adequate investment in diversified sources of new supply. Strong signals from policy makers about the speed of energy transitions and the growth trajectories of key clean energy technologies are critical to bring forward timely investment in new supply. Governments can play a major role in creating conditions conducive to diversified investment in the mineral supply chain.\n\n2. Promote technology innovation at all points along the value chain. Stepping up R&D efforts for technology innovation on both the demand and production sides can enable more efficient use of materials, allow material substitution and unlock sizeable new supplies, thereby bringing substantial environmental and security benefits.\n\n3. Scale up recycling. Policies can play a pivotal role in preparing for rapid growth of waste volumes by incentivising recycling for products reaching the end of their operating lives, supporting efficient collection and sorting activities and funding R&D into new recycling technologies.\n--------------------------------------------------------------------------------\nReport: World's supply of critical minerals for clean energy is concentrated in fewer countries (https://apnews.com/article/3dbee35f17823656b75939305bbd0512)\nciteturn123news16 [wordlim: 100] Published: 1.4 years ago; Report: World's supply of critical minerals for clean energy is concentrated in fewer countries ... \"Critical mineral supply chains can be highly vulnerable to supply shocks, be they from extreme weather, a technical failure or trade disruptions,\" said IEA executive director Fatih Birol.\n--------------------------------------------------------------------------------\n\"There is still much to do\": The world has a critical minerals problem (https://www.axios.com/2024/05/17/mining-critical-minerals-report)\nciteturn123news20 [wordlim: 100] Published: 2.4 years ago; Zoom in: The IEA examines the nitty gritty details of an energy topic du jour: the increasingly thirsty demand for critical minerals like lithium, copper, nickel, cobalt, graphite and rare earth metals that power the \"clean\" energy economy. ... What they're saying: \"The recent critical mineral investment boom has been encouraging, and the world is in a better position now than it was a few years ago, when we first flagged this issue,\" IEA executive director Fatih Birol said in a statement.\n--------------------------------------------------------------------------------\n'A Narrow Pathway': How a New Climate Report Rocked the Energy World (https://time.com/6049584/climate-change-iea-report/)\nciteturn123news22 [wordlim: 100] Published: 5.4 years ago; In 2018, the Intergovernmental Panel on Climate Change, the UN's climate science body, delivered a stark warning from the scientists who study the phenomenon saying that the world was dangerously close to 1.5°C of temperature rise, a point that, once passed, brings the world into a danger zone that could result in irreversible and catastrophic effects such as the disappearance of the Great Barrier Reef. ... The IEA isn't a household name, but the multilateral agency, whose membership consists of 30 countries with advanced economies, plays a key role shaping the global energy business as well as the policies that set the guardrails. ... But in 2020 the IEA ramped up its focus on pushing for emissions reductions, publishing research amidst the pandemic calling for an immediate investment of trillions of dollars in clean energy to help lift countries out of the pandemic's economic rut.\n--------------------------------------------------------------------------------\nIEA analysis charts \"narrow\" pathway to Paris climate goal (https://www.axios.com/2021/05/18/iea-analysis-charts-narrow-pathway-to-paris-climate-goal)\nciteturn123news23 [wordlim: 100] Published: 5.4 years ago; The pathway for transforming global energy systems to reach net-zero emissions by 2050 is \"narrow but still achievable\" and demands unprecedented acceleration away from fossil fuels, an International Energy Agency report published Tuesday concludes. ... Key findings: \"Beyond projects already committed as of 2021, there are no new oil and gas fields approved for development in our pathway,\" the report notes. ... The bottom line: \"The scale and speed of the efforts demanded by this critical and formidable goal - our best chance of tackling climate change and limiting global warming to 1.5°C - make this perhaps the greatest challenge humankind has ever faced,\" IEA executive director Fatih Birol said in a statement.\n--------------------------------------------------------------------------------\n⛏️ Critical mineral mess (https://www.axios.com/newsletters/axios-generate-91fd5240-1389-11ef-8b7b-11b857b2ee97)\nciteturn123news24 [wordlim: 100] Published: 2.4 years ago; Zoom in: The IEA examines the increasingly thirsty demand for critical minerals like lithium, copper, nickel, cobalt, graphite, as well as rare earth metals that power the \"clean\" energy economy. ... What they're saying: \"The recent critical mineral investment boom has been encouraging, and the world is in a better position now than it was a few years ago, when we first flagged this issue,\" IEA executive director Fatih Birol said in a statement. ... Threat level: Given sizzling Atlantic and Pacific ocean temperatures, record global warmth that has persisted for 11 straight months, and the El Niño to La Niña transition, it is perhaps likely that this summer may rival the previous hottest seasons of 2021 and 1936.\n"
